    "\n",
    "**Background: Day 3 (Week 1) lecture recap:**\n",
    "\n",
    "We rarely have access to an entire population, so we collect a **sample** and use it to draw conclusions. Three ideas from Day 3 are central to this module:\n",
    "\n",
    "> - **Sampling methods:** How we choose the sample determines whether it represents the population. Random, systematic, stratified, and cluster sampling each offer different tradeoffs between cost, bias, and representativeness.\n",
    "> - **Sampling bias:** A non-representative sample leads to inaccurate conclusions. Random sampling minimises bias by giving every individual an equal chance of selection.\n",
    "> - **Central Limit Theorem (CLT):** For sufficiently large random samples (n ≥ 30), the distribution of the sample mean approaches a Normal distribution regardless of the population's shape. This underpins almost all of the inferential statistics in Module B.\n",
    "\n",
